    "universal": {
        "taxiNotes": "In cities, metered taxis run roughly Rs 60–80 per km — most drivers refuse the meter for tourists, so agree the price before getting in. Night rides (10 pm–6 am) run ~50% higher. For trek regions, the taxi section below lists the going rate for shared jeeps, tourist buses and mountain flights. Pathao/InDrive show the fare in advance and are the safest option in Kathmandu and Pokhara.",
        "busNotes": "Local green buses are cheapest but crowded; tourist coaches (Greenline, Mountain Overland) cost more and are safer. Shared jeeps (jitney) are the standard on trek roads. Avoid riding on roofs, travelling after dark, and never pay a second driver the same fare twice.",
        "entryNote": "Since 2023 a licensed guide is required for trekking in national parks and conservation areas, and permits are issued through registered agencies. Heritage sites charge a lower 'SAARC' or 'Nepali' rate and a higher foreign-tourist rate — ask for the official ticket booth and keep your ticket for spot checks. Permits are paid in NPR cash on the trail.",
        "scams": [
            {
                "title": "Taxi 'broken meter' overcharge",
                "script": "Driver claims the meter is broken and quotes 2–4× the fair rate (e.g. Rs 1,000 for a ride that should cost Rs 300).",
